<div class="project-copy">
<p class="meta">Participatory behavioural research</p>
<h3>Cookstove adoption in South Africa.</h3>
<p class="research-lead">Instead of a safety lecture, bread. Five women from Dunoon co-designed the intervention over five baking sessions at a community bakery. BREADrev then took it into Dunoon.</p>
<dl class="record" aria-label="Cookstove research">
<div class="record-row">
<dt>Question</dt>
<dd>What influences the adoption of improved cookstoves among households in Dunoon?</dd>
</div>
<div class="record-row">
<dt>Co-design workshops</dt>
<dd><p>Five consecutive sessions in July 2017 at Jedd’s Bakery, BREADrev’s community bakery training school in Fish Hoek, facilitated by Jeremy Barty.</p><ol class="workshop-days">
<li><strong>Session 1.</strong> Coffee and fresh baking, then a high-hydration dough: each step demonstrated, then repeated by each participant. Outdoors, chopping firewood, loading and lighting the rocket oven, and regulating its heat.</li>
<li><strong>Session 2.</strong> The same bread, with little help. An LPG stove and heater demonstrated at lunch, and a fire-safety video.</li>
<li><strong>Session 3.</strong> Each participant lights the LPG stove and makes tea; the oven is fired and run without help.</li>
<li><strong>Session 4.</strong> Scones from each participant’s own recipe, judged against each other.</li>
<li><strong>Session 5.</strong> A final scone bake, tea from the LPG stove, certificates of attendance and exit interviews.</li>
</ol><p>After each session, participants took the bread home to share.</p></dd>
</div>
<div class="record-row">
<dt>Observed in the workshops</dt>
<dd>The oven was new to everyone. “How is it possible that the fireplace is so small without smoke?” one participant asked, and most gathered at its warmth on cold mornings. By the last sessions all five operated it with ease, from recipes they had adapted. Several winced each time they lit the LPG stove.</dd>
</div>
<div class="record-row">
<dt>The pilot</dt>
<dd>From 12 August to 27 September 2017, BREADrev and three trained facilitators ran two sequences in Dunoon: baking scones in an improved biomass oven, and serving tea and scones made on an LPG stove. Each step was designed to engage a motivation, among them curiosity, warmth, the smell of baking, play, saving fuel, sharing, and disgust at paraffin’s smell.</dd>
</div>
<div class="record-row">
<dt>Finding</dt>
<dd>Compared with a control group, households exposed to the pilot showed significant increases in LPG and biomass stove ownership, but no significant reduction in paraffin stove ownership. Eight of the thirteen targeted motivations changed significantly; curiosity, comfort and hunger linked to the biomass oven did not.</dd>
</div>
<div class="record-row">
<dt>Limits</dt>
<dd>A pilot with 44 exposed households, a short timeframe and mainly qualitative data, gathered in English and isiXhosa and later translated. It does not show that adoption lasted.</dd>
</div>
</dl>
<p class="source-note">Co-designed with five women from Dunoon and the Dunoon community representative. Workshops facilitated by Jeremy Barty of BREADrev. Maré, M’Rithaa and Chisin, Sustainability (2023), <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/su15065328">doi:10.3390/su15065328</a>; Maré, doctoral thesis, Cape Peninsula University of Technology (2023).</p>
